WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.019
This Sunday, the South Terminal
at London's Gatwick Airport

00:00:03.019 --> 00:00:05.609
will reopen to the public after
nearly two years closed.

00:00:05.609 --> 00:00:08.210
And I figured that reopening would
just be a case of the team here

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:10.460
turning the lights back on,
dusting off some equipment,

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:12.269
and getting back to business.

00:00:12.269 --> 00:00:14.540
But from talking to the folks
who are getting everything ready,

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:18.100
it turns out that mothballing an entire
airport terminal for 18 months

00:00:18.100 --> 00:00:19.820
and then reopening it again

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:22.720
is much more difficult
than you might think.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:26.019
- In March 2020 when the pandemic struck,
we obviously realized

00:00:26.019 --> 00:00:28.310
there was going to be a
significant downturn in traffic.

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:31.189
So we took the decision to close
one of our terminals.

00:00:31.189 --> 00:00:33.340
Gatwick Airport would generally see

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:37.140
upwards of 900 movements off of
our runway on a busy summer day.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:41.090
During the height of the pandemic, we
were down to two or three flights a day

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:43.440
with certainly less than
500 passengers on certain days.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:44.820
I think it's fair to say,

00:00:44.820 --> 00:00:47.640
in March 2020 we all thought
it'd be over in two weeks' time.

00:00:47.640 --> 00:00:51.230
- If the terminal had been shut down
for just a week, or maybe a month,

00:00:51.230 --> 00:00:53.960
then yes, things would probably
have just kept ticking over.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:56.980
But there's some point at
which upkeep is required.

00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:59.190
In the same way that you're not meant
to leave a car sitting unused

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:00.280
for more than a couple of weeks,

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:03.550
the mechanical parts in things like
the baggage belts and jetbridges

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:06.540
need to be run, tested,
and maintained regularly,

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:09.530
or they might not start up ever again.

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:12.070
Closing the terminal does
reduce costs, yes,

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:13.670
but it doesn't make them zero.

00:01:13.670 --> 00:01:16.150
- There's a number of core services
we've had to keep running.

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:19.430
We had to do regular maintenance
and safety checks on all of our

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:22.050
passenger boarding bridges
and airfield equipment.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:27.010
Water systems, lighting systems,
power, fire, life safety systems,

00:01:27.010 --> 00:01:30.520
everything that's there to look after
the people and keep everyone safe.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:31.520
But things like, you know,

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:36.240
heating and lighting in various areas
nd power for escalators and lifts,

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:37.400
they were all shut down.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:40.890
Mainly to save money during the crisis,
we had to make cuts wherever possible.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:43.950
We've been taking the opportunity
to use the space for filming.

00:01:43.950 --> 00:01:48.310
Film companies have been coming in and
doing various shows around the airport.

00:01:48.310 --> 00:01:50.330
Some of which, I think, will be airing
in the next few weeks.

00:01:50.330 --> 00:01:52.330
A large number of our workforce
had been furloughed,

00:01:52.330 --> 00:01:54.630
and since the 1st of October,
we've had everyone back in

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:56.250
to get the airport back up and running.

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:59.060
Now as passenger numbers start to grow,
we are actively recruiting

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:02.370
and we need to increase our workforce
by a significant number.

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:05.360
We're reopening 85% of it straight away.

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:06.880
We've got a phased plan for our airlines

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:09.539
moving back over from North Terminal
over three days,

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:12.890
but all the infrastructure will come
online more or less from day one.

00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:14.290
In terms of a challenge?

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:16.130
Yeah, it's the biggest one
I've done at Gatwick airport.

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:18.300
It's been open for 60-odd years,

00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:20.230
that's now been closed for the
best part of two years.

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:23.270
You know, buildings don't react
too well to that sort of thing.

00:02:23.270 --> 00:02:24.930
The scale of what
we're doing here is...

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:27.520
we've often likened it to
opening a smaller airport,

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:28.600
completely from scratch.

00:02:28.600 --> 00:02:30.670
It is a huge task that we're undertaking.

00:02:30.670 --> 00:02:32.400
I'm feeling really confident, actually.

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:34.430
I think we're in a really good place to do
it.

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:36.180
- The first flight from the South Terminal

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:38.520
is scheduled to leave on Sunday
in the early hours of the morning.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:42.180
So it'll probably be about 3am when the
first passengers go through the doors.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:44.099
And they probably won't know or care

00:02:44.099 --> 00:02:45.940
that the terminal's only just
opened again:

00:02:45.940 --> 00:02:47.660
they'll just want to get
to their destination.

00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:49.440
If everything goes to plan,

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.970
they probably won't even know
that the terminal was ever closed.

